WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:08.521
In this video we'll learn how to
split complex tasks into a series

2
00:00:08.521 --> 00:00:10.367
of simpler subtasks
by chaining multiple

3
00:00:10.367 --> 00:00:13.137
prompts together. You
might be wondering why would you

4
00:00:13.137 --> 00:00:15.908
split up a task into multiple
prompts when you

5
00:00:15.908 --> 00:00:18.678
could achieve it with one prompt
and chain of

6
00:00:18.678 --> 00:00:21.448
thought reasoning like
we learned in the previous video.

7
00:00:21.448 --> 00:00:24.833
We've already shown that language
models are very good at following

8
00:00:24.833 --> 00:00:26.988
complex instructions, especially the
more advanced models

9
00:00:26.988 --> 00:00:27.603
like GPT-4.

10
00:00:28.565 --> 00:00:31.921
So let me explain why we
would do this with two analogies

11
00:00:31.921 --> 00:00:33.598
comparing chain of
thought reasoning and

12
00:00:33.598 --> 00:00:34.437
chaining multiple prompts.

13
00:00:34.998 --> 00:00:37.382
The first analogy to compare the
two is the

14
00:00:37.382 --> 00:00:39.766
difference between cooking
a complex meal in one go

15
00:00:39.766 --> 00:00:41.090
versus cooking it in stages.

16
00:00:42.032 --> 00:00:44.808
Using one long complicated
instruction can be like

17
00:00:44.808 --> 00:00:47.583
trying to cook a
complex meal all at

18
00:00:47.583 --> 00:00:50.358
once where you have
to manage multiple ingredients,

19
00:00:50.358 --> 00:00:52.092
cooking techniques,
and timings simultaneously.

20
00:00:52.694 --> 00:00:55.764
It can be challenging to keep
track of everything and ensure that

21
00:00:55.764 --> 00:00:58.579
each component is cooked perfectly.
Chaining prompts on the other hand

22
00:00:58.579 --> 00:01:01.650
is like cooking the meal in
stages where you focus on one

23
00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.953
component at a time ensuring that
each part is

24
00:01:03.953 --> 00:01:06.000
cooked correctly before
moving on to the next.

25
00:01:06.722 --> 00:01:08.862
This approach breaks down
the complexity of the

26
00:01:08.862 --> 00:01:11.002
task making it easier
to manage and reducing

27
00:01:11.002 --> 00:01:12.073
the likelihood of errors.

28
00:01:12.574 --> 00:01:14.461
However this approach
might be unnecessary

29
00:01:14.461 --> 00:01:17.922
and over complicated for a very
simple recipe. A slightly better

30
00:01:17.922 --> 00:01:20.754
analogy for the same thing is
the difference between

31
00:01:20.754 --> 00:01:23.586
reading spaghetti code
with everything in one long file

32
00:01:23.586 --> 00:01:25.159
and a simple modular program.

33
00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:29.076
The thing that can make spaghetti
code bad and difficult to debug

34
00:01:29.076 --> 00:01:30.744
is ambiguity and
complex dependencies between

35
00:01:30.744 --> 00:01:34.358
different parts of the logic.
The same can be true of a complex

36
00:01:34.358 --> 00:01:36.582
single step task submitted
to a language model.

37
00:01:37.323 --> 00:01:39.799
Chaining prompts is
a powerful strategy when you have

38
00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:43.100
a workflow where you can maintain
the state of the system at

39
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.576
any given point and take different
actions depending on

40
00:01:45.576 --> 00:01:46.401
the current state.

41
00:01:46.902 --> 00:01:50.735
And so an example of the
current state would be after you've

42
00:01:50.735 --> 00:01:52.652
classified an incoming
customer query the

43
00:01:52.652 --> 00:01:55.207
state would be
the classification so it's an

44
00:01:55.207 --> 00:01:57.443
account question or it's a product
question.

45
00:01:58.325 --> 00:02:00.429
And then based on the state
you might do something different.

46
00:02:01.892 --> 00:02:03.592
Each subtask contains
only the instructions

47
00:02:03.592 --> 00:02:06.991
required for a single state of
the task which makes the system

48
00:02:06.991 --> 00:02:08.973
easier to manage,
makes sure the model

49
00:02:08.973 --> 00:02:12.372
has all the information
it needs to carry out a task and

50
00:02:12.372 --> 00:02:14.638
reduces the likelihood of
errors as I mentioned.

51
00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:17.786
This approach can also
reduce in lower costs

52
00:02:17.786 --> 00:02:20.112
since longer prompts with
more tokens cost more

53
00:02:20.112 --> 00:02:22.438
to run and outlining
all steps might be

54
00:02:22.438 --> 00:02:24.764
unnecessary in some cases.
Another benefit of this

55
00:02:24.764 --> 00:02:27.090
approach is that it
is also easier to

56
00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:30.579
test which steps might
be failing more often or to have a

57
00:02:30.579 --> 00:02:32.905
human in the loop
at a specific step.

58
00:02:32.905 --> 00:02:35.522
So to summarize because
this was a long explanation,

59
00:02:35.522 --> 00:02:38.139
instead of describing
a whole complex workflow in dozens

60
00:02:38.139 --> 00:02:40.756
of bullet points
or several paragraphs in one prompt

61
00:02:40.756 --> 00:02:43.955
like in the previous video,
it might be better to keep

62
00:02:43.955 --> 00:02:46.571
track of the state externally and
then inject relevant

63
00:02:46.571 --> 00:02:47.444
instructions as needed.

64
00:02:48.305 --> 00:02:50.810
And what makes a problem complex?
I think in general

65
00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:52.814
a problem is complex
if there are many

66
00:02:52.814 --> 00:02:54.818
different instructions and potentially
all of them could

67
00:02:54.818 --> 00:02:56.823
apply to any given
situation as these are

68
00:02:56.823 --> 00:02:58.827
the cases where it
could become hard for

69
00:02:58.827 --> 00:03:01.833
the model to reason
about what to do. And as you build

70
00:03:01.833 --> 00:03:03.837
with and interact with
these models more you'll

71
00:03:03.837 --> 00:03:05.841
gain an intuition for
when to use this

72
00:03:05.841 --> 00:03:06.843
strategy versus the previous.

73
00:03:07.464 --> 00:03:10.704
And one additional benefit that I didn't
mention yet is that

74
00:03:10.704 --> 00:03:13.061
it also allows the
model to use external

75
00:03:13.061 --> 00:03:16.007
tools at certain points
of the workflow if necessary. For

76
00:03:16.007 --> 00:03:18.364
example it might decide
to look something up

77
00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:22.488
